d "ff58c76f-d503-592c-8830-047604f3389f" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "56f780de-76f1-5a1a-81ae-abc4bc467c3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332576" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)+5{e}^{4t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=7{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+6{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system of linear ODEs above can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}^{′}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,x(t)+{e}^{4t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+\\,{e}^{5t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-5}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-6}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-7}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-8}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=(-2)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 5+{e}^{5t}\\cdot 0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+1\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 0+{e}^{5t}\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531652 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5af1a84-1b56-54b2-b7ce-8c0d34ffda38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531365} :answer-field/id #uuid "1524671b-66ed-568b-a1ea-32c1c8729504" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531401} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95276f1c-36f0-5f08-904e-b3c36535d237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531187}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531187} :answer-field/id #uuid "1be54abc-d2f8-5e71-86b4-95e3ede8e8b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531408} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "776ac9c0-5765-58c1-ae99-c312dfc60e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531129}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531129} :answer-field/id #uuid "39227e03-733f-5344-954d-3c31f3502f19" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531434} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2f832c5-576b-5b01-8a76-3c3ab8a327a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531218} :answer-field/id #uuid "393e1770-3ee3-5e3e-950e-08a227d928cc" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531435} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb1585ad-6c25-559d-a87d-5cb17b1bf0fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531375}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531375} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f6f012a-2c3a-5777-891a-7086389514fb" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531441} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f2a7653-2cc3-5d49-8385-0aabe06fc0eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531209}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531209} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aed8437-ee45-5890-83bb-09c9f9e1bcc8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531454} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "376594fc-6236-56e8-b6ba-46f5e5522edc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530984}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530984} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e3bde12-1083-598d-9f01-6622ee8cbb56" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531480} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e52aaec-6699-5c73-9493-b586f9420d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531111}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531111} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5d80446-5433-569c-ad43-d2633b905967" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eadcb6eb-28e0-561c-baf8-c3576858d2b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-332585" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe systems of linear ODEs above can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}^{′}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,x(t)+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-5}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-6}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-7}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-8}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=(-3)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+7\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-1)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(-2)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4899d2f0-4a9a-54c0-b255-243f00296fb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x-z,\\,$ $1,\\,$  and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532082} {:answer/id #uuid "7307529a-c5fa-50c3-85e4-0c320e676b56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$yz,\\,$ $1,\\,$  and $z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532192} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3d9025-0e2b-5e52-b2d1-c27a27ac63c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2z,\\,$ $0,\\,$  and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532212} {:answer/id #uuid "900204f4-4ee6-51fa-bded-94c975db4cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2z,\\,$ $1,\\,$  and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532260} {:answer/id #uuid "d5255563-50f7-52ae-bccd-46caade6840e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$xz,\\,$ $0,\\,$  and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532390}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532082} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2718901-61ad-591c-ba8b-c2899ee3b35f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "01a5e642-9803-5a1b-8846-85c812cf1513" :question/math-academy-id "q-162815" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy-zy$ on the intersection of the surfaces $y+z=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,-y⟩=\\lambda ⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ y+z=1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy-zy\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=y+z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨y,\\,x-z,\\,-y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80c94ff5-e094-5fec-a663-783e3db82388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9493" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532225}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532225} :answer-field/id #uuid "efc50829-ddbd-58f0-8a60-4f7a72a20ec5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a330685-db48-5728-bb5b-a1b5a0526e38" :question/math-academy-id "q-332929" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.63),$ compute $P(X<4).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places.\n\n$P(X<4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.63),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.37)}^{x-1}(0.63), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(0.37)}^{0}(0.63)+{(0.37)}^{1}(0.63)+{(0.37)}^{2}(0.63) \\\\  & ≈0.9493.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1426c5a-cb34-58ac-9b8e-e7c4a43242b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532324}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532324} :answer-field/id #uuid "065b0803-ec37-52f0-b035-55be85dd7364" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532493} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f7c4fa-35f3-5ea1-bf8d-567b0e9bd672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531987}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531987} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a8aefaa-4fc2-5f99-957b-9d1dc03b153e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3860fd8d-fa51-5495-8869-9f057f53d9a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-250073" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}$ and the line $y=9x-14$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ Find the values of ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}.$\n\n${y}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{y}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} \\\\ y=9x-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =9x-14 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9x+14 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-7) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=7.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the parabola since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(2)^{2} \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,4).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & ={x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2} \\\\  & =49,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(7,49).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/4e/4e0ce399e32d7b957b71384d61cf08024b5d9995fde28d5f1fac71e1f123b2aa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101089e6-8955-56e5-a9af-e1292704d66d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531964} {:answer/id #uuid "32a2455a-2737-50ee-86bb-360681e30611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532037} {:answer/id #uuid "85e130e6-d2bb-54dd-9b8e-4da5b5c088bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532238} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c11f76-19e6-5543-8fb4-9888b01581c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532307} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3a215d-79d6-530f-8102-b49ccd071024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532374}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532238} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d6ee34c-e06e-5482-9e88-a1329d3b48a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "69684600-8c5e-5874-b78e-2cbd727505f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-155534" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+x$ and the line $y=3x-1$ intersect at a single point with coordinates $(p,q).$ What is $p+q?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+x \\\\ y=3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x & =3x-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-3x+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=1.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. \nLet's choose the equation of the line. Substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-1 \\\\  & =3(1)-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the only point of intersection is $(1,2).$ Therefore, $p=1,\\,q=2,$ and \n\n$$\np+q=1+2=3.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/53/53b8622b0d2fa3fa756d5520d24849fb633539fb471037b9fce6c97464a5fc20.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532672 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a59d5def-358e-53b7-8955-ecdd2b78feb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532306}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532306} :answer-field/id #uuid "75cc18fd-6c53-54eb-b993-7a84046752bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532546} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b65d251-1335-5b26-a787-171e76d4951a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532057} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeeb0417-7377-5df8-aa31-302ace210d7c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "82d663f5-9675-5779-8562-0b4d4248c2b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-250076" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+2x-3$ and the line $y=5x+7$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ Find the values of ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}.$\n\n${y}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{y}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+2x-3 \\\\ y=5x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =5x+7 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x-10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-2$ and ${x}_{2}=5.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =5{x}_{1}+7 \\\\  & =5(-2)+7 \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,-3).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =5{x}_{2}+7 \\\\  & =5(5)+7 \\\\  & =32,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(5,32).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/98/98d656438193d68da44573720da4a0fa5f7e1f7d253c29e0de2e309468c8b552.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532673 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84d4b28b-adba-57ec-9abd-1c6eab03e465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532232} :answer-field/id #uuid "21503cab-a324-5fe0-bffe-94e6d9d3497e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "82d7f69c-d6ad-553d-a4a8-4fc134f8a8d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335528" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.7),$ compute the value of $P(X>5)$ round to $4$ decimal places.\n\n$P(X>5)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.7),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(0.3)^{x-1}(0.7), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X>5.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\nP(X>5)=1-P(X\\le 5)\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4)+P(X=5) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{0}(0.7)+(0.3)^{1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{2}(0.7)+(0.3)^{3}(0.7)+(0.3)^{4}(0.7) \\\\  & =0.99757.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>5) & =1-P(X\\le 5) \\\\  & =1-0.99757 \\\\  & ≈0.0024.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532675 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ce64594-4d44-5fbb-916a-9540f6c9aad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1452" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532092} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc4bd4f-eaf1-5902-990b-f37f8dc13633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2186" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532211} {:answer/id #uuid "d55089b5-7956-5fa0-8147-9220f21301db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1630" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532391} {:answer/id #uuid "e07dadf3-01af-5381-bcd2-159701c2246f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1965" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532413} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c17dfa-72d1-519b-a04e-f7cb863e8d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1406" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532415}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532415} :answer-field/id #uuid "5df74f69-e9ee-5e86-8090-3b562192d1f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "88f1dddc-e661-5d20-9bf7-86d209c9d4bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-97183" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right),$ compute $P(X\\ge 3)$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{x-1}\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right), & x=1,2,3… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X\\ge 3.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{0}\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{1}\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right) \\\\  & ≈0.8594.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & ≈1-0.8594 \\\\  & =0.1406.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "904862d5-897c-534e-9e2f-e61c615561b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532262}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532262} :answer-field/id #uuid "abca62a1-f98f-5e60-8172-1b9e4959acc5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532571} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cef0f64-87f7-5719-93e3-ab9a559f0d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532093} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8833578-9795-544f-95d8-425c9b507a3a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8a3fea1f-f2c7-5a54-bfc4-1db59932f81e" :question/math-academy-id "q-250072" :question/problem "The parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+3x+15$ and the line $y=4x-5$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ Find the values of ${x}_{1}$ and  ${x}_{2}.$\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-{x}^{2}+3x+15 \\\\ y=4x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+3x+15 & =4x-5 \\\\ -{x}^{2}-x+20 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}+x-20) & =0 \\\\ -(x+5)(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-5,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-5$ and ${x}_{2}=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532679 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a8db4e8-f732-5d22-9704-09e0ccf298b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(3t)+\\sin(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532285}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532285} :answer-field/id #uuid "3247c73e-d754-5617-80f3-a0beacefea90" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532515} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f84eb7c6-9806-5d01-aade-ce5ce09883c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532472}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532472} :answer-field/id #uuid "4788b925-6ee1-5536-8ffa-a6478b71d51d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "900adaef-859d-5d14-9981-202d2269e069" :question/math-academy-id "q-334626" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $2\\pm 3\\text{i}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">$2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(2+3\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(2-3\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((2+3\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(2+3\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(2+3\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ (2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+3{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{3t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t+{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6884fc73-9e1c-5600-b1f9-aaba1f71b390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532166} {:answer/id #uuid "7f22556c-3b2e-524d-85bb-92b3a3ca508b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532220} {:answer/id #uuid "841b4151-23c6-5e4c-bcd4-1737cd56ac6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532231} {:answer/id #uuid "b208aace-0a45-5228-9c09-221962d12ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532327} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b32aba-933a-56f5-8b1d-d0dea1394bd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532422}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532220} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bbfb83c-6d7a-5008-8767-3335022b3066" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9485be5f-7e9e-52c2-8dc7-d3a495a119a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-152313" :question/problem "Given that the line $y=4x+k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y={x}^{2}+6x+7,$ what is the range of possible values of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=4x+k$ intersects the parabola $y={x}^{2}+6x+7,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+6x+7 \\\\ y=4x+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+7 & =4x+k \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+7-k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,\\,b=2,$ and $c=7-k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{{2}^{2}-4(1)(7-k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{4-28+4k}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{-24+4k}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24+4k & <0 \\\\ 4k & <24 \\\\ k & <6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abedf875-4b66-5cf2-8796-0df622776e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532318} :answer-field/id #uuid "133c52bf-75f1-5b10-8319-1bbbc0724fa9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532501} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a82dbd44-f08e-5a33-b1b6-6f9773885612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532313} :answer-field/id #uuid "795ff076-e948-5018-81c9-39834569438a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b39dbd28-21a4-5509-83b2-cf250304af27" :question/math-academy-id "q-250085" :question/problem "The parabola $y=-{x}^{2}-4x$ and the line $y=4$ intersect at the point $P.$ Find the coordinates of $P.$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-{x}^{2}-4x \\\\ y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-4x & =4 \\\\ -{x}^{2}-4x-4 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}+4x+4) & =0 \\\\ -(x+2)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=-2.$\n\nWe know the y-coordinate of the intersection point from the equation of the line. So, the only point of intersection is $(-2,4).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/18/187c98be201ec084cdd4fb21f0347f9d768c251cfedcacc9532d754741199e14.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283ebc04-f8a2-5328-b526-0807928a5a14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532014} {:answer/id #uuid "d7787e12-314e-5739-b74c-e63de38ced70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532394} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a9def2-c17c-5098-82fa-fe65f39f5c2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532425} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ac17cb-7070-52ae-a020-36f3ef568515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532426} {:answer/id #uuid "f0dd39fb-017e-5809-bd44-92c3cd0e399c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532453}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532425} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c5c2381-aeb2-5e5f-8fa1-214d94fd8d00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d109f13f-6c22-5d50-a9f4-da3fbec07962" :question/math-academy-id "q-155523" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}-2$ and the line $y=x$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ What is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}-2 \\\\ y=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2 & =x \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+2=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aae80146-7711-5756-a3e1-b69c4a8b8bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532317}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532317} :answer-field/id #uuid "537c7c57-4db6-5792-bbce-5d1fd693dffa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532531} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec28ef51-9620-5155-9c04-545b698f6e12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532443} :answer-field/id #uuid "bef92fe3-e86f-53cd-bf4b-51bb44134db0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dace6513-0d3a-5c62-b857-525a505a8ea4" :question/math-academy-id "q-250156" :question/problem "Given that the line $y=3x+2$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+k,$ find the range of possible values of $k.$\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=3x+2$ intersects the parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+k,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+5x+k \\\\ y=3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+k & =3x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+(k-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=k-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{(2)^{2}-4(1)(k-2)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{4-4k+8}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{12-4k}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative. Setting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-4k & <0 \\\\ -4k & <-12 \\\\ k & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k>3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4be3563b-85a4-5927-8f14-75809dbb4706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3263" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532090} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9c899d-e556-5912-93c1-9e3ca0347975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3074" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532104} {:answer/id #uuid "551c66e8-4471-5f2d-9a69-c55890cd1f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3182" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532116} {:answer/id #uuid "68de0d90-b739-525e-a634-18b6e92914fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2901" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532167} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1a9056-6d3f-565a-ae29-98e576722262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532377}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532104} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd41d07a-8118-5a4f-af31-10c9c686bf80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e7dd6d27-99b8-51e0-8f07-28863587b350" :question/math-academy-id "q-97161" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.1),$ compute $P(4<X\\le 10)$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.1),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.9)}^{x-1}(0.1), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4<X\\le 10) & =P(X\\in \\{5,6,7,8,9,10\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=5)+P(X=6)+P(X=7)+ \\\\  & \\,P(X=8)+P(X=9)+P(X=10) \\\\  & =f(5)+f(6)+f(7)+f(8)+f(9)+f(10) \\\\  & ={(0.9)}^{4}(0.1)+{(0.9)}^{5}(0.1)+{(0.9)}^{6}(0.1)+ \\\\  & \\,{(0.9)}^{7}(0.1)+{(0.9)}^{8}(0.1)+{(0.9)}^{9}(0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.3074.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e79b2f4c-88fd-5171-9805-d4c810b84f1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{124}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532434}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532434} :answer-field/id #uuid "635112d3-93b9-5181-b8a6-34ea068cbead" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ef957d04-4e42-5090-b9a6-8e161a911753" :question/math-academy-id "q-332586" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right),$ compute the **exact** value of $P(X\\le 3).$\n\n$P(X\\le 3)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x-1}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{0}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{1}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65ac0981-1b1d-5c23-9cc0-fc444715eee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533376} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9a387f-3c17-5e04-b07f-ec5133f1dfe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533601}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533376} :answer-field/id #uuid "40c5a737-0591-5fd5-8df7-bba8f7661150" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533833} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70dfdf1e-bde5-58a0-8f24-d392080fc911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533405} {:answer/id #uuid "a6dff475-5ce9-583a-91b1-fe2ebf698ee4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533405} :answer-field/id #uuid "563869eb-e9b6-592a-b3a5-fad42529fb9f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "130e77e3-e68d-5bfc-a672-f433d0d78798" :question/math-academy-id "q-333626" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has only one eigenvalue. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =4$ and it's positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f67469d62d6a0eb61397927924e55868437ad09f405c3b2a9d73567af00c4620.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "230537c8-69a6-557d-91bb-f030f6bb01c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ff/ff337e0120343c7c956afa5e49ea3b482fbd79fe363783a018842d1bfeb340d6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533224} {:answer/id #uuid "335d5523-771d-554d-9156-9e4fbbf3eece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/404c1f68f360e2916a6281033c8a7e9771b648c61156da903ce2f66b931b924e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533263} {:answer/id #uuid "67d912e0-e47b-5590-9c31-8a1b3ebc0a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533383} {:answer/id #uuid "7110c95e-51b1-5868-be7a-c34f3b9a9768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/54eba0116ac434bdadbc878e8bd173b6174a42642cfcfe6825a8bdb178a3282c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533406} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4b1aa4-b78d-5e39-ae30-f82d43e13af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7e48266d30ae7c204b25e6eddca968d1b4ce9cd8e1c7b19cd20247fa66070f0b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533679}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533224} :answer-field/id #uuid "f19bbfd2-a4f0-5fac-92d7-e5b144aac77f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6296be80-55c8-5a2e-8b17-a0c84de2ed61" :question/math-academy-id "q-333296" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-6t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/ff/ff337e0120343c7c956afa5e49ea3b482fbd79fe363783a018842d1bfeb340d6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d7b6960-ec55-5900-899c-bc269a25c30c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533399} {:answer/id #uuid "8787e948-643c-527a-bb41-b899213c3f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533461} {:answer/id #uuid "8a308ba1-a690-5028-bd14-4b4c094a9be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533469} {:answer/id #uuid "c26e2d45-59a8-5ca1-907a-99bb13567a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533608} {:answer/id #uuid "f92eca85-a0a5-5dec-acb0-377d28dc68d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533750}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533399} :answer-field/id #uuid "efdeec04-664e-562e-b615-6cc0242e52ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b213b7e1-d060-5456-8c0f-13babec4bbc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-80502" :question/problem "![](images/15/155e2eac58e35942918ef52682ebfdad4df283409372ac4a54d20218c363704e.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-1,2]\\times [-1,2]$\n\nII. $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534071 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [