fa-badf-a713e9212bf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533268} {:answer/id #uuid "62e4cb92-0825-54c2-ab59-7acd871353ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533367} {:answer/id #uuid "6aec5bdb-6052-5017-8cf2-42fb083477e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533389} {:answer/id #uuid "a8248137-e842-5af1-b6c2-063abb2bc006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533547}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533389} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc4487a4-144f-5b03-86d5-0ad215d73311" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d04cdee5-5005-58d2-afad-7aaebd536510" :question/math-academy-id "q-333601" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[1,\\text{ }-1]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/45/4522e4e6ed382e821aaa47d40a191135161f95eef276b6e8ba3a8514e6bfdfd8.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-7}} | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-8}} | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/db/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a6/a680b8c9319290df3beb20d7db964f385b368b01ead33961e2c10d22b19db4eb.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/45/4522e4e6ed382e821aaa47d40a191135161f95eef276b6e8ba3a8514e6bfdfd8.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.36$ | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | $4.08$ | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/db/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/13/13a57f14ea65118473c24c6eb1dbf50a0461060201ced051bb2eb6776f5daf64.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a54e01d-d3da-520d-a71e-fb2b3d3a8bd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{6t}+e^{-6t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533275}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533275} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ec158fd-889b-5066-b795-fdf52e06926c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533801} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f40c120d-2ef8-574c-b604-70f11f9600d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{6t}+e^{-6t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533740} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ac691a2-04d5-52ef-bc49-8d3327cd84e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f69ba2b0-76ef-5087-a5bf-906e0adf24a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-334388" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">${e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">${e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & 6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-6t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=6t,\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-6t)={e}^{-6t} \\\\ r(6t)={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-6t} \\\\ 6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-12t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-6t}-{e}^{6t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{12t}({e}^{-6t}-{e}^{6t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-6t}+{e}^{6t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-6t}+{e}^{6t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-6t}+{e}^{6t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{6t}+{e}^{-6t}) \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{12t}({e}^{-6t}-{e}^{6t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{6t}+{e}^{-6t} & {e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t} \\\\ {e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t} & {e}^{6t}+{e}^{-6t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16636eaa-e2cc-5e82-a93c-29ae2c9a4b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=4,\\,b=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534317} {:answer/id #uuid "590d032f-044c-52af-affe-ade519cfab6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=1,\\,b=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534445} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9268e1-dbb3-5170-96ed-895c49adebf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=3,\\,b=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534613} {:answer/id #uuid "c40a62c3-bbe0-5d17-9e8f-240d7fe1b737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=2,\\,b=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534704} {:answer/id #uuid "efbe8073-8987-54ae-9846-bf0535624288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0,\\,b=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534798}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534613} :answer-field/id #uuid "172d876b-a2ec-53c9-b98d-a6ec2ea0fdc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0f1e5457-cea5-5c5a-997b-b02818578f46" :question/math-academy-id "q-216060" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a+\\frac{3}{5}b=\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}a+\\frac{1}{7}b=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $5$ and the second equation by $7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a+\\frac{3}{5}b=\\frac{9}{5} & |\\times 5 \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}a+\\frac{1}{7}b=1 & |\\times 7\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5a+3b=9 \\\\ 3a+b=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the second equation can easily be solved for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+b & =7 \\\\ b & =7-3a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $b=7-3a$ into the first equation and then solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a+3b & =9 \\\\ 5a+3(7-3a) & =9 \\\\ 5a+21-9a & =9 \\\\ -4a & =-12 \\\\ a & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $b,$ we substitute $a=3$ into the equation $b=7-3a$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =7-3a \\\\  & =7-3(3) \\\\  & =7-9 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $a=3$ and $b=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e6143a6-0fdd-5669-84d8-0e113cd51643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534620}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534620} :answer-field/id #uuid "22db2219-dd1d-5c73-8420-03acf31177fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534853} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b7dfa22-473e-57b5-a937-ceec1b5ff546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534530}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534530} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cc1d4df-8587-5128-8fdf-3b45c0f0a1dc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2f8f1100-44e9-5de0-ac27-77c13ead7f67" :question/math-academy-id "q-215740" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{1}{3}y=6 \\\\ 5x-y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $3.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{1}{3}y=6 & |\\,\\times 3 \\\\ 5x-y=4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+y=18 \\\\ 5x-y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $y$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-y$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}6x+\\cancel{y} & =18 \\\\ 5x-\\cancel{y} & =4 \\\\ \\hline 11x & =22 \\\\ x & =2\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=2$ into the equation $6x+y=18$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+y & =18 \\\\ 6\\cdot 2+y & =18 \\\\ 12+y & =18 \\\\ y & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=2,\\,y=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68733ae1-b610-5dd9-b95d-12135f00648e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(5(t+2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534477} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e339ad0-7370-56ee-a106-ae32b1e3a838" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534876} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4db09dc0-d4c5-5e09-a70f-eae2e3269be4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(5(t+2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534416}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534416} :answer-field/id #uuid "b890faf2-939e-5590-92f1-af9dc867356d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "530f8a57-b248-5ddf-8ae9-a8b808f3a729" :question/math-academy-id "q-335435" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(-2) \\\\ y(-2)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"130px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t+2)}\\,x(-2) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2)) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2))\\, \\\\ \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2))\\,\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535018 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccf4bfc3-88a6-5d09-ae6d-e269ffac1931" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534724}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534724} :answer-field/id #uuid "32fbac1b-2c0d-5963-85ad-5444d6fe7fc9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534860} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad656808-65b5-5adb-8dd4-7e9667a6904d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534655}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534655} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d296c9e-3e4f-5251-b99c-1087847747be" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "634cc981-026f-5720-a0d6-757e4b38cffe" :question/math-academy-id "q-215834" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+2y=1 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{y}{9}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $9.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+2y=1 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{y}{9}=-1 & |\\,\\times 9\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+2y=1 \\\\ 6x-y=-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Let's solve for $y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-y & =-9 \\\\ 6x+9 & =y \\\\ y & =6x+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $y=6x+9$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2y & =1 \\\\ 5x+2(6x+9) & =1 \\\\ 5x+12x+18 & =1 \\\\ 17x+18 & =1 \\\\ 17x & =-17 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $y,$ we substitute $x=-1$ into the equation $y=6x+9,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6x+9 \\\\  & =6(-1)+9 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1,\\,y=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81b3f5e6-7b2e-56b7-9389-d1e188527eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534547}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534547} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cb22693-eb88-5d68-998f-9fb46b5af777" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534858} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "615d06a8-9c96-51de-bff8-a126ddbcc263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534463}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534463} :answer-field/id #uuid "b535eccc-73da-535c-a127-32c7c5dd2549" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "87b4272d-ed6b-52ae-b49c-9dfe670380e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-216038" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}p-\\frac{1}{4}q=3 \\\\ -4p-7q=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,q=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $12.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}p-\\frac{1}{4}q=3 & |\\,\\times 12 \\\\ -4p-7q=4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4p-3q=36 \\\\ -4p-7q=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $4p$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-4p$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $p$-variable, and allows us to solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{4p}-3q & =36 \\\\ -\\cancel{4p}-7q & =4 \\\\ \\hline -10q & =40 \\\\ q & =-4\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $q=-4$ into the equation $-4p-7q=4$ to solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4p-7q & =4 \\\\ -4p-7\\cdot (-4) & =4 \\\\ -4p+28 & =4 \\\\ -4p & =-24 \\\\ p & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $p=6$ and $q=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0293fcaf-7265-58db-982d-824c6a56e9e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,y=-\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534268} {:answer/id #uuid "5fcca77f-33b4-51d9-b59e-2f22876bcd95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4,\\,y=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534459} {:answer/id #uuid "b1db4db7-8a40-5e37-8cf9-10fa79be6e22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,y=-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534664} {:answer/id #uuid "cd385079-e588-5673-a07b-71dcd96eba0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3,\\,y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534725} {:answer/id #uuid "d2977dc8-5522-59a8-9ba9-e7485deb3f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,y=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534736}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534725} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4d9a375-abbc-5813-a298-e638283c175f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "98e817d9-610e-538f-a711-341d85a62ad2" :question/math-academy-id "q-216035" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{3}{2}y=-5 \\\\ -2x-y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $6.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{3}{2}y=-5 & |\\,\\times 6 \\\\ -2x-y=-2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+9y=-30 \\\\ -2x-y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $2x$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-2x$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable, and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{2x}+9y & =-30 \\\\ -\\cancel{2x}-y & =-2 \\\\ \\hline 8y & =-32 \\\\ y & =-4\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=-4$ into the equation $-2x-y=-2$ to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-y & =-2 \\\\ -2x-(-4) & =-2 \\\\ -2x+4 & =-2 \\\\ -2x & =-6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=3$ and $y=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13574205-8e5b-557d-b506-8f6098c78c23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534307} {:answer/id #uuid "13e49d2a-fab6-5159-b1c0-5a9b594d165b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,-2)$ and a local maximum at $(-1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534311} {:answer/id #uuid "5de0e24f-6b20-5c6e-ace0-a245f8aa51ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has local minima at $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534454} {:answer/id #uuid "7d90fa6d-adf2-5db3-b9b7-4dc01fdc1df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(1,-2)$ and a saddle point at $(-1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534535} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc001e1-be83-5588-9961-a4a046536923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-1,-2)$ a local maximum at $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534688}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534307} :answer-field/id #uuid "b199132b-7b0e-5674-af57-8442f38abdba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9f714ecf-c225-5f6f-850c-327baa1dc6f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-81172" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-4y-4x,$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-4y-4x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-4y-4x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}-4 & 2(xy-2)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4=0 \\\\ xy-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these values into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny=-2\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{-2}=-1,\\,\\,y=2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-4)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-4)=2y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-4)=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-4)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-1,-2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,-2)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-1,-2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,-2)=det({f}^{″}(-1,-2))=0\\cdot (-2)-(-4)^{2}=-16<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(-1,-2).$\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,2)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,2)=det({f}^{″}(1,2))=0\\cdot 2-{4}^{2}=-16<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35563bcf-877b-5a37-b29d-40f490c08f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$  has a saddle point at  at $(1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534367} {:answer/id #uuid "58b65e9f-4d99-5093-aa0f-4d2897f123b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum  at $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534443} {:answer/id #uuid "87ca91c3-a26a-5635-add5-c073ea764a5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum  at $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534560} {:answer/id #uuid "c14d984f-72ff-59f8-bdd6-8c050d73fad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum  at $(0,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534697} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ae0c36-9a90-5251-835e-e8a464a23a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum  at $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534560} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f7fa629-9baf-529b-99af-39063b4618f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ac06f66c-0484-5c24-8d99-9d11ac2dbda8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81048" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4x & e-{e}^{y}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x=0 \\\\ e-{e}^{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-4x=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the second  equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ne-{e}^{y}=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$   \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(e-{e}^{y})=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(e-{e}^{y})=-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{y}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -e\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))=(-4)\\cdot (-e)-{0}^{2}=4e>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,1))_{11}=-4<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06af144f-4c5a-5f7d-bcae-becd399595a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s=1,\\,t=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534272} {:answer/id #uuid "35184dd6-b1fe-5c13-bdb5-273a2cadca99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s=3,\\,t=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534366} {:answer/id #uuid "80d413a7-4a8a-5e3d-8f60-ce6ec9e391fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s=2,\\,t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534543} {:answer/id #uuid "90a54c3b-d57a-5b56-b610-4e5ba4b8c320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s=0,\\,t=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534586} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f2c462-fa61-5493-90fc-306d1d3339be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s=-1,\\,t=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534711}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534272} :answer-field/id #uuid "86ae1002-c318-5989-8c0c-03e862059522" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cb34e92b-11ce-5586-a6db-ed20acf39bd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-216062" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}s-\\frac{2}{3}t=-1 \\\\ s+\\frac{1}{4}t=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}s-\\frac{2}{3}t=-1 & |\\times 3 \\\\ s+\\frac{1}{4}t=2 & |\\times 4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5s-2t=-3 \\\\ 4s+t=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the second equation can easily be solved for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4s+t & =8 \\\\ t & =8-4s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $t=8-4s$ into the first equation and then solve for $s:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5s-2t & =-3 \\\\ 5s-2(8-4s) & =-3 \\\\ 5s-16+8s & =-3 \\\\ 13s & =13 \\\\ s & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $t,$ we substitute $s=1$ into the equation $t=8-4s$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =8-4s \\\\  & =8-4(1) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $s=1$ and $t=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535081 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53744223-8cd7-552e-a59b-eb16d6ea99ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8,\\,y=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534430} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ccee43-d730-5189-89d6-32f56294f8d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8,\\,y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534677} {:answer/id #uuid "d224f1c2-daea-5840-bde5-ea7d182fed3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3,\\,y=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534735} {:answer/id #uuid "d6375d72-3485-5e65-848c-9b62e50dff15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=12,\\,y=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534749} {:answer/id #uuid "fa92e62f-e86c-521d-82dc-8c0da4ab37e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4,\\,y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534819}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534430} :answer-field/id #uuid "49233c73-c921-5e3f-b3db-c183bb683e95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ecc873f4-57df-56ae-b5fa-d73b11c36cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-216177" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}x+2y=-6 \\\\ -x+\\frac{1}{3}y=-10\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $4$ and the second equation by $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}x+2y=-6 & |\\times 4 \\\\ -x+\\frac{1}{3}y=-10 & |\\times 3\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+8y=-24 \\\\ -3x+y=-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. We can eliminate $x$ by adding the two equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{3x}+8y & =-24 \\\\ -\\cancel{3x}+y & =-30 \\\\ \\hline (8y+y) & =(-24-30) \\\\ 9y & =-54 \\\\ y & =-6\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=-6$ into the first equation to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+8y & =-24 \\\\ 3x+8(-6) & =-24 \\\\ 3x-48 & =-24 \\\\ 3x & =24 \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=8,\\,y=-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65f2d77a-2b81-5d83-98b3-76968aebd21b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535595} {:answer/id #uuid "98f7f5ac-92f2-57d4-9f68-6f000ab5715b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535675} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7ae81f-0d85-559d-8d81-82c0fa4336b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535702} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fc64ad-4252-5b4b-bd30-9d46db806f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535738} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d75dd9-891a-59ff-9bb8-4426ce367ae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535782}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535702} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8a55fbf-ed96-50fd-a31b-6db6c54e9cf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0b671036-c3a4-5237-8125-3af3f125754a" :question/math-academy-id "q-125779" :question/problem "![](images/14/14ca2d4167ff6950d5a9d7bb41cc856ecbc211a033518b8b366ac977953700b1.png)\n\nThe point ${P}^{′}(3,1.5)$ is the image of the point $P(-4,-2)$ under a dilation with the center at the origin, as shown above. What is the scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "A dilation with center at the origin and a factor of $k$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(kx,ky)\\,.\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $P$ and equating the result to ${P}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k\\cdot (-4),k\\cdot (-2)=(3,1.5)\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-4k=3 \\\\ -2k=1.5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle k=\\frac{3}{-4}=\\frac{1.5}{-2}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$   \n\n![](images/d3/d3b818151bcfddd3fd4f642463ba4e06ba1844d4b772652dadde0d3049e441ee.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535946 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7ddbe0-edeb-5af4-acdc-0e8bdedd7785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535455} {:answer/id #uuid "2637c6d9-f699-5e24-af7a-329870be2fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535495} {:answer/id #uuid "369283f1-0003-5dfd-b8bf-780c8fe3b4e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{4}t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535518} {:answer/id #uuid "512a7ed1-b0bf-5dd9-9978-7ed2decafc3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535559} {:answer/id #uuid "82e84ea1-476d-5fa4-8ca5-a30056317436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{3}{8}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535642}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535495} :answer-field/id #uuid "709bb002-42ed-5076-9f07-4d7555384dbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "476be737-90ba-5bfe-8a20-c650319e8b3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106414" :question/problem "![](images/89/8934e5b6135b97b845dad9a6b87056d2c228fd2424d3a72141df694d46ccf7c0.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-6,12).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-6,12),$ we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=12$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6=2pt \\\\ 12=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =2pt \\\\ -3 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =p{\\left(-\\frac{3}{p}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)t,\\,y=\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535950 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4f64bf3-528b-508d-afe7-7fb41040696f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos(t)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535760} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0cddfae-ecf5-50fd-9bfc-04c2102bb55f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5cb555db-6a59-509d-947e-27c4f1a2a7e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-335062" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">$2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535952 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34dc9e34-88b2-585e-b8f0-9828d32d21e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535515}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535515} :answer-field/id #uuid "852f5335-7100-5af8-83e3-1bcac38ee757" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535879} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "792a7020-748e-5273-a5c8-4d48fd7db315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535622} :answer-field/id #uuid "f288405a-29b1-5b71-8025-b48b0179b10a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5daa6476-7e34-5e5c-afc2-27c877b6a525" :question/math-academy-id "q-280220" :question/problem "Determine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-8,8).$ Express your answer in the form $x=2pt,y=p{t}^{2},$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-8,8),$ we can substitute $x=-8$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-8=2pt \\\\ 8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{4}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{\\left(-\\frac{4}{p}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{16}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{16}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(2)t,\\,y=(2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=4t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e595558-a7b5-5d7e-a16f-ecdb52280ea7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535463} {:answer/id #uuid "227a8863-645d-5b6a-927f-2a72b437610f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535488} {:answer/id #uuid "568f958f-3170-5ebb-9dbd-ad93f7c5fe07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535572} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b59232-9050-5b0c-8245-2c74284bbb90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535686} {:answer/id #uuid "eb27dbfa-ca02-5d9c-abad-838dbe761243" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "196" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535798}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535572} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c91a14a-bb0b-5009-a73f-c0026c98e31c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c4ed7da6-2468-5aec-962f-cfbcbb62e8fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-18697" :question/problem "Let $(x,y)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $x-y?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.04x-0.08y-4=0 \\\\ 2x-3y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the top equation by $100,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.04x-0.08y-4=0 & |\\,\\times 100 \\\\ 2x-3y=4 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-8y-400=0 \\\\ 2x-3y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ we will get a $-4x$ term in the second equation that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-8y-400=0 &  \\\\ 2x-3y=4 & |\\,\\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-8y-400=0 \\\\ -4x+6y=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{4x}-8y-400 & =0 \\\\ -\\cancel{4x}+6y & =-8 \\\\ \\hline (-8y+6y)-400 & =-8 \\\\ -2y & =392 \\\\ y & =-196\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=-196$ into the equation $2x-3y=4$ to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y & =4 \\\\ 2x-3(-196) & =4 \\\\ 2x+588 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =-584 \\\\ x & =-292\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-292$ and $y=-196.$ So, \n\n$$\nx-y=-292-(-196)=-292+196=-96.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536000 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2728a05c-b733-5d7a-a3ee-efdd536081bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535496} {:answer/id #uuid "84d0281f-81cf-5464-888b-3fb42539c91d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535646} {:answer/id #uuid "d216a963-8190-5dc8-93bb-49be306033dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535756} {:answer/id #uuid "f89943d7-a9b6-59a5-95a6-1c63bacee9df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535816} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe0850c-bd3b-535d-bfb0-aada34fa3333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535646} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e101cd2-0992-5de6-9b2d-4f35a8656511" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dd60924f-af00-5e97-9e77-c29eb2bc8035" :question/math-academy-id "q-18855" :question/problem "Let $(x,y)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $x+y?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.05x-0.03y=0.04 \\\\ 0.4x+0.2y=0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the first equations by $100$ and the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.05x-0.03y=0.04 & |\\times 100 \\\\ 0.4x+0.2y=0.1 & |\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y=4 \\\\ 4x+2y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $3,$ we will get a $-6y$ term in the first equation that can cancel with the $6y$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y=4 & |\\,\\times (2) \\\\ 4x+2y=1 & |\\,\\times (3)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-6y=8 \\\\ 12x+6y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}10x-\\cancel{6y} & =8 \\\\ 12x+\\cancel{6y} & =3 \\\\ \\hline (10x+12x) & =(8+3) \\\\ 22x & =11 \\\\ x & =\\frac{11}{22} \\\\ x & =0.5\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=0.5$ into the equation $4x+2y=1$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2y & =1 \\\\ 4(0.5)+2y & =1 \\\\ 2+2y & =1 \\\\ 2y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=0.5$ and $y=-0.5.$ So, \n\n$$\nx+y=0.5+(-0.5)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15c607d4-a926-5be5-9a79-37c070202dbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536318} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1012f6-ac99-5e4e-a9f6-7a1a7f4d837b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536381} {:answer/id #uuid "4dff97b4-92fa-577d-9da9-9c6d62f99972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536413} {:answer/id #uuid "9648dece-0d06-5613-b589-cdd0fc59e342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536524} {:answer/id #uuid "ced0514e-753d-5d75-af21-617042730647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536608}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536318} :answer-field/id #uuid "b39c4d94-12fb-5a15-b6bb-bc1e15c955c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "76343f99-bb5f-5787-863a-2557d071c09b" :question/math-academy-id "q-88408" :question/problem "The point $P(2,3)$ is rotated ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about the origin $O$ and then translated according to the function $(x,y)↦(x-p,y+q).$ If the image of $P$ under this sequence of transformations is $(-1,5),$ then $p+q=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\nA rotation of ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about $O$ can be represented by \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(-y,x).\n$$\n\nApplying this to the point $P,$ we get\n\n$$\n(2,3)↦(-3,2).\n$$\n\nNow, applying the translation to $(-3,2),$ we get\n\n$$\n(-3,2)↦(-3-p,2+q).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the resulting point is $(-1,5),$ by equating the $x$- and $y$-coordinates, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-3-p=-1 \\\\ 2+q=5\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}p=-2 \\\\ q=3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\np+q=(-2)+3=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537893 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a5189d-540f-5143-b180-eea39c520ccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and $(2,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537228} {:answer/id #uuid "1b84224c-2aef-5acf-9174-2e514405a489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537285} {:answer/id #uuid "1d898613-60f0-545a-a43b-ab2df0a31df5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537292} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc2dfcf-d574-5c58-9c7c-5fd06dbeeee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ and $(1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537473} {:answer/id #uuid "aab67e8e-c1fd-5b7a-a619-a599bce9e1a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537552}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537228} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c0ebced-cf33-5e38-93fa-adaeaf32a0f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1ecf42ce-06ef-5bfd-b762-67d3f1697496" :question/math-academy-id "q-40946" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e1892e632e2485b17400ba9d2a9372bf164e20812c3b6fe4f9b03ab1348fab7d.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=f(x) \\\\ y=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The solutions $(x,y)$ of the system correspond to the intersection points of the two curves.\n\n![](images/8d/8dfab4aa640e596227bd6a6a09af36264f6bce9bc0906a69ea433d6e6a3db78d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, the curves intersect at \n\n$\\,$ $(-1,1)\\,$ and $\\,(2,1).$ \n\nTherefore, these are the solutions of the corresponding system."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f5bbd1a-b6f0-537f-a093-4b3d1be271cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1)$ and $(-1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537393} {:answer/id #uuid "57e6a003-f73c-515c-a06f-ba516c1963bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-3)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537409} {:answer/id #uuid "6d485cb2-89c5-5022-b5b4-533fc087f5b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,3)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537441} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc80659-76af-5a9d-b845-d8d0123dc768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537496} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fdda5c-b3a2-5a24-9521-9f6e08ac1fa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537441} :answer-field/id #uuid "f66e49fb-db34-54b1-8488-6385f866a2fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e75342f3-2efc-5d3d-a3c3-d44c19646c04" :question/math-academy-id "q-78292" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6831eff5d8feb87ebba8e7d449d98fbe72c3ed4df01d8374d4866e8e3456ddc.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-x+1 \\\\ y={x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The solutions $(x,y)$ of the system correspond to the intersection points of the two curves.\n\n![](images/e1/e1ebbb0ce147dc986dd4d47539f055407c9b870af2ada9629520c4fa1d9aef33.png)\n\nFrom the graph, the curves intersect at \n\n$\\,$ $(-2,3)\\,$ and $\\,(1,0).$ \n\nTherefore, these are the solutions of the corresponding system."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16da06c8-5818-5d83-b103-468e96d457f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538281} {:answer/id #uuid "28af7c9a-9d9e-5376-9c5b-6d7fa5e689d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,1)$ and $(1,2,-7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538317} {:answer/id #uuid "84475504-c2ca-5622-a840-6d61ac41fdbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538534} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9f2d3f-0ff9-53c5-b3cd-a63b7c043f47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2,9)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538607} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7c6f53-ab06-5a01-be5d-aa83ddab04f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2,9)$ and $(1,1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538317} :answer-field/id #uuid "560ffd1b-5a84-5953-b5a7-cc2f4832a155" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "278532bd-d5b2-5391-ac62-5dd37f8bae10" :question/math-academy-id "q-129553" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z=8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩\n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =24{x}^{2}-12y \\\\  & =12(2{x}^{2}-y) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =-12x+3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3({y}^{2}-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨12(2{x}^{2}-y),\\,3({y}^{2}-4x),\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}12(2{x}^{2}-y)=0 \\\\ 3({y}^{2}-4x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=2{x}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({(2{x}^{2})}^{2}-4x) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{4}-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{3}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions of the last equation are $x=0,1.$ So, we have two cases.\n\n- For $x=0,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({0}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({0}^{3})-12(0)(0)+{0}^{3}+1=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our first point is $(0,0,1).$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({1}^{2})=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({1}^{3})-12(1)(2)+{2}^{3}+1=-7.\n$$\n\nSo, our second point is $(1,2,-7).$\n\nTherefore, the points where the tangent plane is horizontal are $(0,0,1)$ and $(1,2,-7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0174e176-0cb7-5b47-a8ba-9fe3b08757a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538238} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2d612f-1d4f-54a0-829f-81ec557baecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538402} {:answer/id #uuid "53c58a6b-8263-5f64-84ba-2acd0232972e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538412} {:answer/id #uuid "623914d6-e6b8-5dc3-8327-81f1d2919a84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538452} {:answer/id #uuid "fce51717-00cf-5470-b72d-97e68246b052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538452} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce54675-4c82-511c-b17c-ef8ba86365cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9820b99c-2acb-5f80-b231-9670a302c372" :question/math-academy-id "q-37402" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(2)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{8}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540067 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bd683e8-a847-5e05-99c1-b681ee2fc285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539659} {:answer/id #uuid "7027ff52-37a6-5339-8ae8-c81614622435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|x{|}^{3}+2|x{|}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539668} {:answer/id #uuid "a982a159-987e-5602-86d9-3a2e2fddf4d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539767} {:answer/id #uuid "abed5605-656d-5242-a084-ad7f11b0d696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539771} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3b5131-7b3a-5bc8-9252-83975f1ec410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539879}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539879} :answer-field/id #uuid "149df211-1ed2-522f-a2c8-98bc68e1b2c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "899a46d0-c395-5e11-8fbc-04b90f756929" :question/math-academy-id "q-341407" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+8$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{3}-2(-x)^{2}+8) \\\\  & =-(-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+8) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541057 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff5d65f0-c6de-5048-a2be-21f61c684329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4t}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540941} :answer-field/id #uuid "428707b6-8d94-52b6-9362-47c3559ec433" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540972} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e2e4b8f-9b19-50ef-87e0-50189c923190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e^{4t}+2e^{2t}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540765}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540765} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbe9fab9-0798-54e1-b0d4-366304913f18" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0823a96b-ef1d-57d5-85ea-8ef149bb4b9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333132" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{2}(t)-4,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{2}{s-2}-\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{1}{s},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{1}{s}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2}{s-2}-\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{1}{s}\\} \\\\  & =2\\,{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-2}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2\\cdot t}-{e}^{4\\cdot t}+1 \\\\  & =-{e}^{4t}+2{e}^{2t}+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{1}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\cdot t}+1 \\\\  & ={e}^{4t}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{4t}+2{e}^{2t}+1 \\\\ {e}^{4t}+1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f4b480b-46bc-5ad0-b2dc-179d612978aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2s^2-2s-6}{s(s-1)(s+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540741}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540741} :answer-field/id #uuid "1abf10fb-79e5-50b9-8eca-b49f881c098e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "24075bbd-fad1-5e70-847c-3b96960e39c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-333072" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)+3,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},$\\,$\n${X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3}{s(s+1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-{x}_{2}(t)+3\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}+3L\\{1\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+2={X}_{1}(s)-2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-0=-{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{3}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\},$ and we have used the fact that $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}.$\n\nNow, solving the second equation for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{2}(s) & =-{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{3}{s} \\\\ (s+1){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3}{s} \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3}{s(s+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the first equation and solving for ${X}_{1}(s)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+2 & ={X}_{1}(s)-2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =-2-2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =-2-2\\left(\\frac{3}{s(s+1)}\\right) \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-2s(s+1)-6}{s(s+1)} \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-2({s}^{2}+s)-6}{s(s+1)} \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-2{s}^{2}-2s-6}{s(s+1)} \\\\ {X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-2{s}^{2}-2s-6}{s(s+1)(s-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system in terms of parameter $s$ is \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{-2{s}^{2}-2s-6}{s(s-1)(s+1)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3}{s(s+1)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d2f5dd4-e22e-536b-8ce7-2a9b4757513e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540577} {:answer/id #uuid "706ad2cc-287a-563f-aecf-c4e029c86155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540723} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff278d4-c3af-52d0-a261-2ce96bee14a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540768} {:answer/id #uuid "afc25060-6cdc-5f7a-b06c-abd717564cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540822} {:answer/id #uuid "d25821c3-b2f1-5f34-b7c8-4715d5113d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540822} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9538eb7-9f5e-5704-8c29-873db219be68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3115c643-189e-5d0d-96c2-b1b8ae304036" :question/math-academy-id "q-74419" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(3,2)$ and the line $y=x+1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](images/80/80f3e7d79f84a3894e563c78747e22ccf11c889783c39ed6fb688a4242160d60.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=x+1$ and passes through the point $(3,2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =-1(x-3) \\\\ y-2 & =-x+3 \\\\ y & =-x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=x+1 \\\\ y=-x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =-x+5 \\\\ x+x & =-1+5 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ into either of the two equations gives $y=3.$ So, our solution is $x=2$ and $y=3.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(2,3).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(3,2)$ and $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-3)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(3,2)$ and the line $y=x+1$ is $\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541090 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27dab55e-2a07-5559-adf9-474a787ae042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (-\\pi /2,0)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (0,\\pi /2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540597} {:answer/id #uuid "4a503419-3b03-55f7-a026-c71a3cdf1fda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (-\\pi /2,0)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (0,\\pi /2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540660} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8aa907-71bd-57bd-b9b0-a791585844c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (-\\pi /2,\\pi /2)}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y))\\text{ }\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540878} {:answer/id #uuid "db274e58-8e5f-5077-88fc-c9024b1f6057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (-\\pi /2,0)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540901} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3ec6df-aa0b-5131-ba4c-022b02da3f60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (-\\pi /2,0)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (0,\\pi /2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,\\text{d}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540940}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540940} :answer-field/id #uuid "c22f973c-4187-5d34-928b-a206bc95c78a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "66ff7371-5323-5876-b071-49b4ca2a6463" :question/math-academy-id "q-80674" :question/problem "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y),\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<\\pi ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<y<0, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y),\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<\\pi ,\\,0<y<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (-\\pi /2,\\pi /2)}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the rectangle $R$ can be represented as a union of two smaller rectangles, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =(0,\\pi )\\times ((-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)\\cup (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =((0,\\pi )\\times (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0))\\cup ((0,\\pi )\\times (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (-\\pi /2,0)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (0,\\pi /2)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (-\\pi /2,0)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (0,\\pi /2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22f7cf30-3ed7-5793-9002-3529283cccb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540587} {:answer/id #uuid "937d33d5-a8a7-5da9-bb11-1ede9ac95d6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{8}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540775} {:answer/id #uuid "b544aa9a-5b87-55cf-b2e4-1e042d4dc7a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540830} {:answer/id #uuid "e847e91b-a66b-5b3c-a257-23c792bc4fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540922} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f4f8f6-66d2-56a3-a1e0-b35ad6aa0fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540923}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540923} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5a7caf1-f1b3-50b7-8338-9e475499f496" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "683e7ef8-4750-58e2-a136-8e19dd7482de" :question/math-academy-id "q-74423" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bc1432b521a372c9a7e264700cc58eef96c34b347e0300f5b7c323066ed0cc7b.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=\\frac{4}{3}x$ that is closest to the point $(-1,7).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{4}{3}x$ and passes through the point $(-1,7).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{4}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-7 & =-\\frac{3}{4}(x+1) \\\\ y-7 & =-\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{4}{3}x \\\\ y=-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}x & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}x+\\frac{3}{4}x & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\frac{25}{12}x & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{25}{4}\\cdot \\frac{12}{25} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=4.$ So, our solution is $x=3$ and $y=4.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(3,4).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=\\frac{4}{3}x$ that is closest to the point $(-1,7)$ is $(3,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541095 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23c48aa5-451f-5b58-9fdf-d8472558f09b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540593} {:answer/id #uuid "4abf62fc-86eb-5564-b600-98ba0a148dab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540661} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8a67c4-b331-5d37-8678-fa4fd7b2a874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540689} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc39615-cf6d-548a-a9c6-d33a2dc2a0ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540738} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4de4cf-7e82-5f05-9f5b-966287d3a5af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540871} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6527b4b-120d-5aff-a1f0-c36c16a9c694" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6ce9a2b1-181d-53a4-9a85-7b3316309f0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140823" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=5.$ Computing $A-5I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541097 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "360b3e1c-48bd-528c-9d5d-938043d6c2b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540614} {:answer/id #uuid "3f31d9f9-5c07-5bf0-9421-ad3c02ba0764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540634} {:answer/id #uuid "582fa1a5-35f2-55f1-ba22-a7eb59f9b896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540677} {:answer/id #uuid "88eadd3f-76f1-5d70-87a5-4c7bc2dd9b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540758} {:answer/id #uuid "d71fd4ba-85b7-5c46-8cbc-f5708e1bd047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-1,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540614} :answer-field/id #uuid "36a01c9a-6a75-5d02-8751-2d076edf1a6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ef129d9-0a92-5a3f-b637-51eadd473818" :question/math-academy-id "q-156055" :question/problem "What is the solution to the following system of inequalities?\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3<4 \\\\ 3-2x\\le 9\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the system, we need to find the values of $x$ that satisfy both $x+3<4$ and $3-2x\\le 9$ simultaneously.\n\n- First, we solve $x+3<4\\,:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & <4 \\\\ x+3-3 & <4-3 \\\\ x & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/c2/c20ffc0279a42a429797f42fcd929fb4dfb499e311dd3ed7b6b61fd95729953c.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve $3-2x\\le 9\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-2x & \\le 9 \\\\ 3-2x-3 & \\le 9-3 \\\\ -2x & \\le 6 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-2x) & \\ge (-1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 2x & \\ge -6 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & \\ge \\frac{-6}{2} \\\\ x & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ae/ae72342a32ea8a952a3d00aec7a8ccc381804ae4b593ba58113911c4bc4b2c09.png)\n\nTo find the values of $x$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/b7/b7d4814919a8c0b2a2ed8e130c8af2bdce5e7fd2d23d10e50e4e293d88170c2f.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThus, the final solution is $x\\in [-3,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3be87dbe-29dc-5043-9a79-a86b2fd5a880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540628} {:answer/id #uuid "4c39f4de-1a6a-52cf-8624-125d851fe285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540664} {:answer/id #uuid "635a77b6-fd8f-5cf2-8d7f-1598dc609b88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540696} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1c0892-2c18-5576-a828-53b1cfdefbaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540717} {:answer/id #uuid "781f5f51-a2bb-5ef5-9600-e203136f5055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540730}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540628} :answer-field/id #uuid "1932c306-3c23-58d3-a967-76e055fcd6ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "777e8b7e-3aad-5700-9be0-3920699b2b4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-114342" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(8,1)$ and the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](images/52/52bfc27007c57351cdf90d88d444676266edc5d2704314db0d99fd9dca8e367f.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2$ and passes through the point $(8,1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-8) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-12+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2 \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2}x-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}x+2 & =\\frac{3}{2}x-11 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{3}{2}x & =-11-2 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-13 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=6$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-2.$ So, our solution is $x=6$ and $y=-2.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(6,-2).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(8,1)$ and $(6,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(6-8)^{2}+(-2-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(8,1)$ and the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2$ is $\\sqrt{13}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69712d4c-92b7-5f9d-abcd-ca7ad46c6567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-s^2+10s+4}{s(s+1)(s+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540707}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540707} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8de0228-5ef5-53a6-b77b-949275d4025a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ca91093-1f1e-55da-a457-e3e1666bf899" :question/math-academy-id "q-333069" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{2}(t)+1,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}} $,\\,$\n${X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3s+1}{s(s+2)}$ ." :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-2{x}_{2}(t)+1\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+4L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}+L\\{1\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-3=-2{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{1}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\},$ and we have used the fact that $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}.$\n\nNow, solving the second equation for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{2}(s)-3 & =-2{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ (s+2){X}_{2}(s) & =3+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ (s+2){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3s+1}{s} \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3s+1}{s(s+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the first equation and solving for ${X}_{1}(s)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+1 & =-{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =4{X}_{2}(s)-1 \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =4\\left(\\frac{3s+1}{s(s+2)}\\right)-1 \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{4(3s+1)-s(s+2)}{s(s+2)} \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{12s+4-({s}^{2}+2s)}{s(s+2)} \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-{s}^{2}+10s+4}{s(s+2)} \\\\ {X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-{s}^{2}+10s+4}{s(s+2)(s+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system in terms of parameter $s$ is \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{-{s}^{2}+10s+4}{s(s+1)(s+2)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3s+1}{s(s+2)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541146 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bf162e4-cd7b-54b0-8466-f4b821b2abe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540629} {:answer/id #uuid "58c4a563-279d-570a-a388-2ff52a0b29f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (1,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540679} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4c177a-706d-56c0-a404-7bc45e333979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540681} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c8fe3f-912f-5186-af72-dca875ba83b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540837} {:answer/id #uuid "dedbb23f-f487-5d15-9528-e264d7f421c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540908}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540679} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b901c12-2a57-5d11-9bb4-941a5ba8e6d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e3894629-c728-5b78-885d-1ca88944c278" :question/math-academy-id "q-156096" :question/problem "What is the solution to the following system of inequalities?\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+4>6 \\\\ 4-x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the system, we need to find the values of $k$ that satisfy both $2x+4>6$ and $4-x\\ge 2$ simultaneously.\n\n- First, we solve $2x+4>6:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & >6 \\\\ 2x+4-4 & >6-4 \\\\ 2x & >2 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & >\\frac{2}{2} \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/6d/6d3b61147527a632ccb46629a356e9f5c92a1ddfc8a52f3d0890cd46eb56f564.png)\n\n- Then, we solve $4-x\\ge 2:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-x & \\ge 2 \\\\ 4-x-4 & \\ge 2-4 \\\\ -x & \\ge -2 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-x) & \\le (-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ x & \\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/14/1452391c950b051c4cf454723498e6fa657f6d5f1315f78e50f3450bf7fbc1eb.png)\n\nTo find the values of $x$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/a2/a22a207cc64d33470e27d0b1f45ad0b87106fafd67b88c2163524a44951639bf.png)\n\n    \n\nThus, the final solution is $x\\in (1,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "359dac84-023f-5ac4-b1e5-2b024191e8ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{8t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541532} {:answer/id #uuid "557ae54f-10b2-58ab-9e71-0c628ea5f179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{8t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541593} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4ad265-f473-5a67-8913-d792566b24af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541815} {:answer/id #uuid "d11ef4d7-b160-5577-b380-46de80da0930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-8t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541866} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c0af83-088a-5ec4-9706-445e6d105d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541932} :answer-field/id #uuid "f94becd7-b475-5661-836a-13b2396bb899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ebc3a86d-4663-502d-a702-2a2557f07e8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334779" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=8y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-8x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22fabb63-36cb-5c3f-98b6-b4f2b8a1b771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543688} {:answer/id #uuid "290abe19-a5e8-5f16-ac8a-3c254b59ca02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}1\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543689} {:answer/id #uuid "a87eed07-0b8a-5520-9c6e-109e5f040429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543712} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fcfdca-6265-5fab-9691-6ac1a660390c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543717} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf036c2-6040-5864-81c8-69e069735579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543724}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543724} :answer-field/id #uuid "04b50b83-c4ee-50ea-ae8a-d5cc1f4efc8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "06448e69-2e5e-5172-aee5-b3151a22cc6e" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/4f/4ff66220bf03e9c5dde372e20aa060fa26a4a57ab481a61845726fde8ca25046.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{2}+(y-2)^{2}&=4\\\\(r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta-2)^2&=4\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta+r^2\\sin^2\\theta-4r\\sin\\theta+4&=4\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)-4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r^2-4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r(r-4\\sin\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/88/882f7487d40a724c331aebb3dd954b8beace88ff5a2e65b4b2b9abedd74885dc.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=0$ and $\\theta=\\pi.$\n\n![](images/41/410279d76b3d76135133c63fe19003839b783a5d4177aff6744bd950fcbe93bd.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le\\theta\\le \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0afc24b7-4e9b-5fc1-a813-41887ebcebbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543681} {:answer/id #uuid "0ffe947f-52e1-584d-be76-77ece40d3f41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543682} {:answer/id #uuid "196f500a-a2af-50e3-9dd1-0ecadbc6d54a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543685} {:answer/id #uuid "2273c691-6884-5960-9489-5d738197d00e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}1\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543687} {:answer/id #uuid "e21ac4be-6b11-5399-80cc-05afed3bd495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543722}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543681} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9146589-ac3d-5d74-94be-b61e9289276d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7d4f7134-3d22-5cbc-8e83-54b913ada494" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+y^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/86/866f3162cd881e997f28cea68fe22fc90b8ef011b6d8beff00ef38d9a69d2eb9.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+y^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+y^{2}&=4\\\\(r\\cos\\theta-2)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2&=4\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta-4r\\cos\\theta+4+r^2\\sin^2\\theta&=4\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)-4r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r^2-4r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r(r-4\\cos\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/6c/6c841a1dc73b8a68ba8d51bb4d0f8d1ff051f8d1c0bafdfdbfac134ccbc651aa.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\n![](images/08/083360143d05d210dcbd11ed86c3a5cc0f2510beeecb68a210e9f52632609867.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15cbb851-3dfc-5f92-8728-a8b8eec6ed2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543684} {:answer/id #uuid "21ffbbe1-7ca1-5c19-9aef-3f70c88274e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543686} {:answer/id #uuid "298cf0ac-ab43-5882-91a6-e7348499b76f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543690} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7adafb-1edd-5777-af5d-4c1cdf3fb5bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543695} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2bb1be-7c1c-50a0-8af1-048b30c61a71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}1\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543715}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543686} :answer-field/id #uuid "c09a0523-101d-5678-835e-b363f24d33b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9021eafc-a91a-5e03-85b9-0baff1b49b29" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+2)^{2}+y^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/3a/3a77e854cc9553675752e3e4e231c8915d390091a4f4fa22cee2eb5ffe279c3e.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(-2,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x+2)^{2}+y^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+y^{2}&=4\\\\(r\\cos\\theta+2)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2&=4\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta+4r\\cos\\theta+4+r^2\\sin^2\\theta&=4\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)+4r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r^2+4r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r(r+4\\cos\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/a9/a934a520dfc672601e31dd73fc71a06505f41b6088325a30b49f4d7d99d0f0d7.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\theta=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}.$\n\n![](images/d5/d5b43bd12ba3ca3b3dd69560c728f9c7b16d2d8331702cc3921e8457f53c294f.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3543a771-464b-542c-9335-538c3709149f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}1\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543691} {:answer/id #uuid "4528835f-33a6-5ec5-9a47-a775cb974623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543696} {:answer/id #uuid "487a6fa0-57bb-500c-9371-92da4be661ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543697} {:answer/id #uuid "86580f35-f153-55b3-b4bc-2e64e0c0b121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543706} {:answer/id #uuid "ac2b1f00-489c-598a-af58-abeb3809e74d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543697} :answer-field/id #uuid "980036b8-3088-5457-9263-9a3e9604d20c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b2b48fc3-a0dd-5bed-af7d-8c8aed9c00ae" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-3)^{2}+y^{2}\\le 9\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/74/74735ff47608d314c82b66367404f6d2bb79c0c1e34b034083ebcd2d74dc55c0.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $3$ centered at $(3,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-3)^{2}+y^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+y^{2}&=9\\\\(r\\cos\\theta-3)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2&=9\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta-6r\\cos\\theta+9+r^2\\sin^2\\theta&=9\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)-6r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r^2-6r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r(r-6\\cos\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/8b/8bafd3851f0378006c6e851b9bb60ab4bbcb1dadafffe4d5b5082d304ca7fed7.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\n![](images/cc/ccdbd4ea144224d5793d4c48018147ca939cba611e5ea38ac649295218ca2f07.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4acdd0df-c785-59f2-9658-4e3a960a5cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3\\cos θ}r^{3}drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543698} {:answer/id #uuid "5031c2db-393e-5fdd-8e60-3997b7fff3b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3\\cos θ}r^{2}\\cos 2θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543701} {:answer/id #uuid "96c17cb2-2129-5e02-9bdf-4663faa9470d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}r^{3}drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543708} {:answer/id #uuid "a19c980b-b72c-5250-b5c3-5c4d8d144b94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3\\cos θ}r^{3}\\cos 2θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543711} {:answer/id #uuid "e5698084-b769-5ac6-b65b-adb1e670787d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}r^{3}\\cos 2θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543723}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543711} :answer-field/id #uuid "aafcfd12-6869-527e-91e6-ff53db154e0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d3a13c3d-8749-55b1-8f45-ee637a70eea2" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle D = \\{(x, y): (x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} + y^{2} \\le \\frac{9}{4}\\},\n$$ then $$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)(x^{2} - y^{2})dA =\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/d0/d01daaf1f81b8dd2b2ab8d01bbbe5a7dd1f4262eb7de9d3977b8c1e104a90ec0.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ centered at $(\\frac{3}{2},0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+y^{2}&=\\frac{9}{4}\\\\(r\\cos\\theta-\\frac{3}{2})^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2&=\\frac{9}{4}\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta-3r\\cos\\theta+\\frac{9}{4}+r^2\\sin^2\\theta&=\\frac{9}{4}\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)-3r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r^2-3r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r(r-3\\cos\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/ac/ac0374d74edf7f4490f7c160b929b9dc5d03d1324d7cb79dc691a5ca21248e35.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\n![](images/c6/c674039d133057398ff5fd98770479e9fba7866524ead09c78adba2c2ac5cb80.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (- r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "010f0bb3-cfce-57dc-a3dd-0e6bf16e313e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543678} {:answer/id #uuid "4cde6b07-0e2a-568b-abd2-30c5e1ac404b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543699} {:answer/id #uuid "63130042-5901-5034-86fc-435727d0f3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543702} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee5d5f6-e473-541e-87b4-e95658ad2cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543705} {:answer/id #uuid "f0fe3285-6e13-5d53-953e-91a811e1db98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543725}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543699} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d84f478-b5b5-5f25-9156-e2d90cf503dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dc21b297-d346-5eac-82b2-8f472263113f" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}\\le \\frac{9}{4}\\},\n$$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/07/074eb42aff2c78d93136fc9e56b2420450f72c5d34c6b12da8bf84e080db75ea.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ centered at $(0,\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{2})^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/ce/cea2b2ddd6b211073b579d71bd8ed08405fa04e354825a124acb71bedf413295.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/bb/bbe07b71b3c82cde2e9175b06904905206984b04ca9a8ccd746b051212f5e8d8.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154fe4ca-a591-51af-b5a1-03adc5261ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543683} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc7ec97-c47c-54d7-b497-f40e8148a957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543692} {:answer/id #uuid "3e0c7a54-ad42-5b46-9438-a5e86783d08d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543694} {:answer/id #uuid "4d85821c-4168-5a6a-994e-4c67538a143d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543700} {:answer/id #uuid "9f993769-4242-5c50-a578-e44a67fa0267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}1\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543692} :answer-field/id #uuid "6397bb0a-9d78-551e-a3e8-a73e2230a72a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fa53fde6-b454-5423-91b4-30fdb6c5d5b2" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/57/57ffd44d5406aafa448571f9a56eebded9a9916def605c08eb4eb1ad1e0df56c.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-2).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{2}+(y+2)^{2}&=4\\\\(r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta+2)^2&=4\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta+r^2\\sin^2\\theta+4r\\sin\\theta+4&=4\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)+4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r^2+4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r(r+4\\sin\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/0b/0b3138e7b6599e8174ccd46648b334b18a505757b845e53af59d0b98e3b4c92b.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=\\pi$ and $\\theta=2 \\pi.$\n\n![](images/11/11dcaf5545227d8231eef23ee29ce52723be103c8a209a578927cacb83d56384.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\pi\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45bde8d9-4c6e-5ee0-940a-5de511005e14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8647" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544241} {:answer/id #uuid "70f23a81-9ddb-5bb1-b52a-a90210c0f213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8677" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544338} {:answer/id #uuid "75826e41-5f0a-5951-9d1b-962bf48353ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8596" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544348} {:answer/id #uuid "896a0afd-8d66-500b-9100-28316a76192d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8467" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544383} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc1e1f6-fe38-5508-ad8f-639f19a1b5e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8417" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544241} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf80934d-3233-573f-8300-6e5e2ddf5899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1455aec3-db48-5efe-b5bf-59b8688bf438" :question/math-academy-id "q-132483" :question/problem "On a webpage, visitors arrive continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $15$ seconds.  Determine the probability of waiting for the next visitor for less than $0.5$ minutes.  Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since visitors arrive continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next visitor appears using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time between two consecutive visitor arrivals. Since visitors arrive on average every $15$ seconds, the rate is $\\lambda =60÷15=4$ visitors per minute.\n\nThe time between two consecutive visitor arrivals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =4,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(4).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{-4x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of waiting for the next visitor for less than \n$0.5$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<0.5) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}4{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4x}{|}_{0}^{0.5} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.8647\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544796 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d461234f-027a-557e-9d34-651597fcb58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2(s+2)}{s(s-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544537}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544537} :answer-field/id #uuid "337fbbd0-2104-528d-b75a-5b62951f2ab9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "229cdf1d-b683-5de3-b351-27b1d6a26233" :question/math-academy-id "q-332026" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{2(2s-3)}{s(s-5)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{2{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-3L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+3L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-4=2{X}_{1}(s)-3{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+2=-2{X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nRewriting the system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(s-2){X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s)=4 \\\\ 2{X}_{1}(s)+(s-3){X}_{2}(s)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{-2-(s-3){X}_{2}(s)}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s-2)[\\frac{-2-(s-3){X}_{2}(s)}{2}]+3{X}_{2}(s) & =4 \\\\ (s-2)[-2-(s-3){X}_{2}(s)]+6{X}_{2}(s) & =8 \\\\ -2(s-2)-[(s-2)(s-3)-6]{X}_{2}(s) & =8 \\\\ -({s}^{2}-5s){X}_{2}(s) & =8+2(s-2) \\\\ -s(s-5){X}_{2}(s) & =2s+4 \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{-2(s+2)}{s(s-5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${X}_{1}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-2-(s-3){X}_{2}(s)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-(s-3)\\cdot \\frac{-2(s+2)}{s(s-5)}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2s(s+5)+2(s-3)(s+2)}{2s(s-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2s-3)}{s(s-5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{2(2s-3)}{s(s-5)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{-2(s+2)}{s(s-5)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a7dd673-0f1c-53a4-bf23-823dac58c3bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544212} {:answer/id #uuid "71a909e6-6f08-5712-bc83-1b6bad8b11a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544339} {:answer/id #uuid "76d4658d-76b6-5b33-aa64-aa0bb388f498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544351} {:answer/id #uuid "ab42cc66-3ec7-5fba-ba3f-33b3a69e4979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544454} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3e916a-1d77-5240-920d-86d1b61a5940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544594}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544351} :answer-field/id #uuid "4824d498-2640-5207-a210-a8d05507618a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "427792fe-a52b-5a35-ba4b-43ed95fe05d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-143573" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{2x+3} & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x+1\\le y\\le x+2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(Y<2\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$ \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<2\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{4}) & ={\\int }_{1+x}^{2}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x=\\frac{3}{4})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1+3/4}^{2}\\frac{2y}{(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+3)}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{7/4}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}[{y}^{2}]_{7/4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}(4-\\frac{49}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544823 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f7f5d76-1e7f-57ed-8313-e71643d50b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}+x}{2y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544158} {:answer/id #uuid "63ff8546-8d92-58c2-a62b-b0c34bccc272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y+x}{2(y+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544302} {:answer/id #uuid "75565937-5318-5439-b48d-e16cca219a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}+x}{2({y}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544347} {:answer/id #uuid "af8c864f-3d09-5723-889c-3b0bde2faabe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y+x}{{y}^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544459} {:answer/id #uuid "f451b045-2660-545d-8769-46b5280b5182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}+x}{y+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544618}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544347} :answer-field/id #uuid "2568d719-6258-502f-b42f-c59bbe997028" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5ffa5cf0-3e04-5a20-bfc9-964d09fea30a" :question/math-academy-id "q-145091" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}({y}^{2}+x), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{8}({y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{3x}{8})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{16}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{12}{16}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}({y}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}({y}^{2}+1), & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{8}({y}^{2}+x))}{(\\frac{3}{4}({y}^{2}+1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}+x}{2({y}^{2}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}+x}{2({y}^{2}+1)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad88c67b-7dd2-5b78-a6b2-b39c461d1394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544457}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544457} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dec9ee2-df88-5435-a628-2db834f23d11" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544671} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdf95821-0667-5470-9d72-b2c2cd72e470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544642}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544642} :answer-field/id #uuid "43acf565-70e8-59c8-9da0-7f2d6c9509f6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544679} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fa7dfaa-3002-551e-b29b-b70703cbc0de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544433}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544433} :answer-field/id #uuid "62b0c5b0-195a-52a8-a42e-0d7cdd505876" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544695} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86aa83a1-0b94-57bd-b3df-ddec044a5317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544378}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544378} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8e0339e-584d-50e3-9d66-c4b5654e80f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9de0e52e-4e54-5cd5-8e58-4fda732ef76e" :question/math-academy-id "q-332388" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{2}{s-2},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=-\\frac{2}{s-2}+\\frac{1}{s-5}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{2t}+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{5t}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2}{s-2}\\} \\\\  & =2{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-2}\\} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2\\cdot t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{2}{s-2}+\\frac{1}{s-5}\\} \\\\  & =-2{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-2}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-5}\\} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2\\cdot t}+{e}^{5\\cdot t} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2t}+{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{2t}+{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac5709fe-0354-5d44-b05a-02a21b0da97c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544456} {:answer/id #uuid "d25214fe-4395-53a2-b548-fff04525e163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6605" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544534} {:answer/id #uuid "dbff2e28-f33a-5f18-8f52-e274e4c55978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6665" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544561} {:answer/id #uuid "e8872e6f-ba5a-570a-bbd3-ab8d4b6496ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6153" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544588} {:answer/id #uuid "fba8fc04-0fa8-577c-bd40-5fbcbbc648c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6565" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544456} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6c361c4-eefd-5311-9a84-4a9a4e2f34ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bc8f4760-f931-5737-8a9c-5aab0b30c4bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-132558" :question/problem "The waiting time for one customer to be served at a cafeteria varies continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate of one person served every $4$  minutes. Determine the probability that the next person is served at least $2$ minutes after the previous one. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the waiting time for one customer to be served at the cafeteria varies continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until serving the next person as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time needed to serve the next person. The time needed to serve the next person can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since one person is served every $4$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right).\n$$\n\n In this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x/4}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next person will be served at least $2$ minutes after the previous one is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/4}{|}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-2/4} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.6065.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6acad7a-a7d5-50fe-94f8-03cc12e4ce79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544448}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544448} :answer-field/id #uuid "0abaee99-8d00-5498-8655-f03c7de5849a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544655} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "357de87f-e9e1-5bed-9740-cf1a1c066776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544205}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544205} :answer-field/id #uuid "43cb3dab-7c67-5df5-bb7f-1b8561a9585b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544680} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ba96b2e-684a-5f93-b08a-1f0e2229610c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544324}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544324} :answer-field/id #uuid "67bc3df3-21d6-5ff8-936a-39854725ffb5" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544700} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c0a2880-de85-52e8-85de-0969492cdb58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544217}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544217} :answer-field/id #uuid "df0f9134-af7b-577f-bb8b-2009b066b22f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d0737b10-0304-5e2e-a18d-ef1cf02a07ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-332382" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{2}{s-3},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s-3}-\\frac{3}{s-1}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{3t}+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{t}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2}{s-3}\\} \\\\  & =2{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-3}\\} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3\\cdot t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-3}-\\frac{3}{s-1}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-3}\\}-3{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-1}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\cdot t}-3{e}^{1\\cdot t} \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}-3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}-3{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544883 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a9d88d-8478-534d-b20a-f7e25d74ee09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2s+10}{s^2-2s+7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544093} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1988ed4-093d-51e3-9639-cb7ca3f1716c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d4b0fddb-f441-5332-9904-2f366a824fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331950" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{4(s-2)}{{s}^{2}-2s+7}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+3L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-2={X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-4=-2{X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nRewriting the system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(s-1){X}_{1}(s)-3{X}_{2}(s)=2 \\\\ 2{X}_{1}(s)+(s-1){X}_{2}(s)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{4-(s-1){X}_{2}(s)}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s-1)[\\frac{4-(s-1){X}_{2}(s)}{2}]-3{X}_{2}(s) & =2 \\\\ 4(s-1)-(s-1)^{2}{X}_{2}(s)-6{X}_{2}(s) & =4 \\\\ 4(s-1)-[(s-1)^{2}+6]{X}_{2}(s) & =4 \\\\ 4(s-1)-({s}^{2}-2s+7){X}_{2}(s) & =4 \\\\ -({s}^{2}-2s+7){X}_{2}(s) & =4-4(s-1) \\\\ -({s}^{2}-2s+7){X}_{2}(s) & =-4s+8 \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{4(s-2)}{{s}^{2}-2s+7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${X}_{1}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{4-(s-1){X}_{2}(s)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-(s-1)\\cdot \\frac{4(s-2)}{{s}^{2}-2s+7}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4({s}^{2}-2s+7)-(s-1)\\cdot 4(s-2)}{2({s}^{2}-2s+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{s}^{2}-8s+28-4(s-1)(s-2)}{2({s}^{2}-2s+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4s+20}{2({s}^{2}-2s+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(s+5)}{{s}^{2}-2s+7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{2(s+5)}{{s}^{2}-2s+7},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{4(s-2)}{{s}^{2}-2s+7}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18eb1c41-b6a4-592b-856a-3cab685afd5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{y-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544140} {:answer/id #uuid "4d9e076f-f8bc-5761-b656-c6602ea176a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544254} {:answer/id #uuid "c9c771ea-0ad2-5a82-b2a7-6dd9f49a0d79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{y}-y}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544509} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0a0f68-ff1b-5054-bdc9-b7e07c3e9fbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(y-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544524} {:answer/id #uuid "fefcf60e-8515-57a8-ab6a-297915943a05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{y}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544644}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544254} :answer-field/id #uuid "b608e08c-09ce-5c00-b348-3354afe78844" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e1e5b64b-413b-5b25-a4f6-89eb28f41500" :question/math-academy-id "q-143644" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6, & \\,y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{y}^{\\sqrt{y}}6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot x\\,{|}_{y}^{\\sqrt{y}} \\\\  & =6(\\sqrt{y}-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6(\\sqrt{y}-y), & \\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6(\\sqrt{y}-y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}-y}, & \\,y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544889 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f44278-1870-59da-b693-ab5e9edbb7b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1349" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544092} {:answer/id #uuid "2fbb0c6a-76c4-5add-8d12-dd2f98f7de84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1990" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544191} {:answer/id #uuid "59c37b7d-f2a8-5b03-96d5-27b94d5bddda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1909" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544285} {:answer/id #uuid "e5302346-1455-507d-8c19-5af7fa10783e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1099" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544580} {:answer/id #uuid "eee1f799-c2f5-5fbc-b0b8-69720cd98bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1299" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544606}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544580} :answer-field/id #uuid "74158e50-3525-5d26-bf97-113e74a7d386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e3898571-1060-5a54-af07-b5592b492d4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-132552" :question/problem "On a busy waterway, barges pass by a particular point continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $10$ minutes. Determine the probability that the next barge will pass between $5$ and $7$ minutes after the previous one. Round your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since barges pass continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time between two successive barges using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time between two successive barges. Since a barge passes every $10$ minutes, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10}$ barges per minute.\n\nThe time interval between two successive barges can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next barge will pass between $5$ and $7$ minutes after the previous one is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5<X<7) & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/10}{|}_{5}^{7} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-7/10}+{e}^{-5/10} \\\\  & ≈0.1099\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "596675b4-21d2-5ac4-b0a2-4839e83555d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8647" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544284} {:answer/id #uuid "5ccd3213-2b5a-598b-9a24-9bb6f2190670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8785" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544289} {:answer/id #uuid "d687dca3-0bd3-526a-a4ab-509c47139640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8980" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544547} {:answer/id #uuid "daa90ef2-43bc-5481-8ed8-ea54977434e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8892" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544558} {:answer/id #uuid "e7be2a9b-7b4a-533e-9a2f-8958ec2a1213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7923" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544284} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4dfd42d-e350-5855-be90-2f0889abb592" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eddfe563-295a-5298-9f24-7cdb158be9e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-132486" :question/problem "A monitor issues a warning signal when an action is needed as part of a production process. Signals occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $0.2$ minutes.  Determine the probability of the next signal occurring within the next $0.4$ minutes. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the monitor issues a warning signal continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next signal appears using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time between successive signals. Since the monitor issues a warning signal every $0.2=\\frac{1}{5}$ minutes, the rate is $\\lambda =5$ signals per minute.\n\nThe times between two successive signals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =5,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(5).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-5x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of the next signal occurring within the next $0.4$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<0.4) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}5{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-5x}|}_{0}^{0.4} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.8647\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545795 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6d2cea6-2dfb-5470-bdb9-b125b8517442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545558}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545558} :answer-field/id #uuid "44ef42ba-8904-590e-855b-cefdf44b2505" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545698} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac5c1f87-7097-5e55-8673-290f95420717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545515}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545515} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece49f7d-e08d-5601-b02d-77ccc0ccc997" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "072c9b75-cb6d-571a-a32a-79cae44e115d" :question/math-academy-id "q-323087" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec1381a50c493fce0f5017e706251c68c1a8a20ba5b68ef268d5905aa20772e6.png)\n\nThe circles $r=4\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of the point $A?$ Express the polar angle $\\theta \\in [{0}^{∘},\\text{ }\\,{360}^{∘})$ in degrees.\n\n$(r,\\theta )=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘})$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},\\text{ }\\,{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={45}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={135}^{∘}.$ \n\nAlso, notice that $r=4\\sqrt{2}$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(4\\sqrt{2},{135}^{∘}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e5b7fb9-337e-502c-9a40-f13cabc0424e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545407}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545407} :answer-field/id #uuid "1438285a-c5cd-5a31-b715-b35536c03825" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545680} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f94ae38d-ef49-51f3-be98-ef3bdb8f6786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545656}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545656} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e3de602-9b02-5e62-a90c-937f84dbf69b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545690} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b32348d-3761-5d58-acac-43ca4b05fbb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545342}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545342} :answer-field/id #uuid "603497ab-4fdc-5c48-88e2-3ed5a2fed9cd" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545714} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3452fc1-db2a-5214-9f07-294a1399cd20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545500}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545500} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c973c0b-c17f-575d-ba6c-439b72f6ffd9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2156ef1c-7aad-5ba1-895f-5a7013935e5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-325647" :question/problem "![](images/82/823ff7d93c17556b8587da62430c3168bbd6d49da43e88450f0804d83598f23f.png)\n\nThe circles $r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=1$ intersect for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively? Express the polar angles ${\\theta }_{A}$ and ${\\theta }_{B}$ in radians.\n\n$({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$),\\,$\n\n$({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(${{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\nAlso, notice that $r=1$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are\n\n$$\n({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(1,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}),\\,({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(1,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$"}]}
